  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Double consonants",
     "texto": [
      "In Italian, double consonants (**doppie**) are pronounced with more tension and length than single ones: **penna** (pen) doesn't sound the same as **pena** (pity); **notte** (night) doesn't sound the same as **note** (notes). The difference is phonological and distinguishes meanings."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "The final written accent",
     "texto": [
      "In Italian, a written accent is compulsory on words of more than one syllable, stressed on the last syllable, that end in a vowel: **città**, **perché**, **caffè**, **lunedì**, **università**. Also on some one-syllable words to avoid confusion: **è** (is, from the verb *essere*) versus **e** (conjunction *and*); **sì** (yes) versus **si** (pronoun)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "tabla",
     "titulo": "Minimal pairs and written accents",
